Behaviour (the public API is unchanged; the new functions and the module `prim` are private):
 prim  Each wrapper is the original expression with the same arguments, so each call site
       computes what it computed before. `rsplit('/')` always yields at least one item, so the
       `unwrap_or(s)` of last_slash_segment is never taken and its `?` in the original never
       returned. `split_slash`/`split_percent` copy the parts into `String`s with the same
       bytes; `percent_decode_utf8` returns `Some` exactly when `decode_utf8` is `Ok`, with the
       same characters. One difference: `split_slash`/`split_percent` collect all parts into a
       `Vec<String>` (24 bytes per element on the translated 64-bit target), which the original
       call sites do not (H5, H6; patch-induced premise 2 below).
 H1  `Sha256::digest` returns the same 32 bytes as `[u8; 32]`. `write!(digest, "{byte:02x}")`
     writes two lowercase hexadecimal digits, high nibble first, zero-padded; `lower_hex_digit`
     maps 0..=15 to `0-9a-f`, so the two pushed chars are the same. Writing to a `String` never
     fails, so the removed `expect` never fired; the capacity reserved is unchanged.
 H2  `is_ok_and(|segment| segment.nfkc().eq(name.nfkc()))` is `true` exactly when decoding
     succeeds and the two NFKC character sequences are equal (`Iterator::eq`), which is the
     `Vec<char>` equality of the `match`; NFKC is pure, so computing `nfkc(name)` only in the
     `Some` arm changes no value. One difference: `prim::nfkc` collects each NFKC form into a
     `Vec<char>` (4 bytes per char), where `Iterator::eq` streams both forms and stops at the
     first difference, so the patched copy panics with "capacity overflow" when a form would
     exceed `isize::MAX` bytes, which the original never allocates. Unreachable in practice:
     NFKC expands a char to at most 18 chars (UAX #15; U+FDFA, 3 UTF-8 bytes), so it needs a
     name or decoded segment of more than of the order of `isize::MAX / 24` bytes. Because of it the Lean
     model of K13 needs a premise that the original would not (the NFKC length bound of
     `prim::nfkc`; formal/trusted/evidence/README.md). The `while` loop over `files` computes `any(..)` with the same
     short circuit. The second loop visits the files in the same order, performs the same
     `manifest_resource_key`, `seen.insert` and `is_sha256_digest` calls, and stops at the same
     first error, which is returned with the same fields; without an error the result is
     `Ok(())` as before.
 H3  `contains(['?', '#'])` is `find(['?', '#']).is_some()`; `strip_suffix('/')` with a char
     pattern and with the one-char string "/" remove the same byte; the rest is unchanged.
 H4  The loop sees the same `(index, ch)` pairs in the same order and returns at the same
     point. The difference is that the whole NFKC form is collected into a `Vec<char>` before
     the loop instead of streamed: the same values, but, as for H2, the patched copy panics
     with "capacity overflow" for a form of more than `isize::MAX` bytes, which the original
     never allocates (unreachable in practice: a name of more than of the order of `isize::MAX / 24`
     bytes), and the model of K13 needs the same NFKC length premise.
 H5  `relative.split_at(..)` and the prefix and suffix tests are unchanged. `split('/')` then
     `next_back()` gives the same last segment as `pop()` on the collected segments, and the
     remaining segments in the same order. `resolve_segments` applies the same step to each
     remaining segment in order ("." skipped, ".." pops and yields `None` on an empty vector,
     anything else is pushed); once `None` it stays `None`, so the result is `None` exactly
     when the original returned `None` and otherwise the same segments (as `String`s). The join
     and the appended suffix are unchanged. One difference: `prim::split_slash` collects every
     segment into a `Vec<String>` (24 bytes per element), where the original iterates
     `split('/')` lazily and collects only the resolved segments into a `Vec<&str>` (16 bytes
     per element), so the patched copy panics with "capacity overflow" when 24 times the
     number of segments exceeds `isize::MAX`, where the original does not (patch-induced
     premise 2 below; unreachable).
 H6  The encoded `Cow`, its first split at `%` and the early return are unchanged; the parts
     after the first `%` are the same, visited in order, and `push_normalized_part` is the
     original loop body; `is_ascii_hex` is `bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_hexdigit())` and
     `is_unreserved` the original `matches!` test. One difference: `prim::split_percent`
     collects the parts into a `Vec<String>` (24 bytes per element), where the original streams
     `split('%')` without allocating, so the patched copy panics with "capacity overflow" when 24
     times the number of parts exceeds `isize::MAX` (patch-induced premise 2 below;
     unreachable).
 H7  The same `strip_prefix` call.
 H8  `Cow<str>`'s `Hash` and `Eq` are those of the borrowed `str`, and `String`'s agree with
     them on the same bytes, so `insert` reports a duplicate for the same keys.
 H9  `!=` on `String` is the negation of `==`; the two branches are swapped with the test.
Patch-induced premises (the Lean statements may need them; the original would not):
 1  NFKC length (H2, H4; K13): the bound `4 * (ExtModel.nfkc t.toList).length ≤ maxStringBytes`
    of `nfkc_spec` for the strings `t` passed to `prim::nfkc` (see H2).
 2  Split vector length (prim, H5, H6; K13 through manifest_resource_key and
    normalize_percent_encoding, K16 through normalize_percent_encoding): the premise
    `hlen : 24 * (splitBytes sep s.val).length ≤ maxStringBytes` of `split_slash_spec` and
    `split_percent_spec` (`sep` = `/` or `%`). Unreachable on the translated target
    x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu: `isize::MAX` is 2^63 - 1, and a virtual address has at most 57
    bits (5-level paging; 48 with 4-level), so every `&str` has fewer than 2^57 bytes and at
    most 2^57 parts (one more than its separator bytes), and 24 * 2^57 = 3 * 2^60 < 2^63 - 1.
    Exhausting memory while copying the parts aborts the process in both versions and is not
    modelled.
